  <!-- Abstract Lead -->
  <p class="lead">
    Next-generation multi-messenger sky surveys (Vera C. Rubin LSST, LIGO-Virgo-KAGRA O4/O5, IceCube, DESI) generate millions of transient candidates per night. Follow-up facilities are severely constrained: scarce 8m-10m spectrographs (Gemini GMOS, Keck, VLT) observe fewer than 0.01% of triggered alerts, wide-field optical imagers (DECam, Rubin) face expansive gravitational-wave localization skymaps (&Delta;&Omega; ~ 100–1000 deg&sup2;), and multi-object spectrographs must assign apertures under acute stellar contamination. Standard alert brokers suffer catastrophic overconfidence on ambiguous boundary targets, permanently squandering finite observing night-hours on false alarms.
  </p>

  <!-- Interactive Model Embed -->
